>
  <p>
    Pool fences are the one fence project where "close enough" is not good enough, because the purpose is safety.
    That is also why we are careful here. Some sources quote a single number for pool-fence height. We looked for that number in the St. George City Code chapter that governs fences and retaining walls
    (Title 10, Chapter 18) and could not verify a residential pool-barrier requirement there. Rather than repeat a figure we
    cannot back up, this page tells you what we do know and how to get the authoritative answer in one phone call.
  </p>

  <div class="sgfw-callout">
    <strong>What we did not verify, and why it matters.</strong> Pool-barrier rules can come from more than one place,
    such as the adopted building code, city amendments, and other health or safety rules, and they can differ for new pools
    and existing pools. Because we have not confirmed which applies to a home like yours, we do not publish a height. Ask the
    city, and get it in writing.
  </div>

  <h2>What we do know</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>A pool fence is still a fence.</strong> St. George City Code §10-18-1 sets height and placement limits for fences generally, including a decorative, open-style requirement for fences close behind the sidewalk in the front yard. Those are fence rules, not a pool-barrier standard, so do not assume a fence that satisfies them is compliant for a pool.</li>
    <li><strong>Front-setback fences need a permit.</strong> Under §10-18-5, a permit is required before you construct any fence or retaining wall in a front setback, unless it is shown in detail on an approved site development plan.</li>
    <li><strong>Retaining walls have their own rule.</strong> If your pool sits on a slope with a retaining wall, §10-18-5 also requires a permit for CMU, segmental block, rockery, and reinforced-concrete retaining walls of 4 feet or greater.</li>
    <li><strong>Your HOA may add rules.</strong> Style, color, and height are commonly regulated, and HOA approval does not replace anything the city requires.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How to get the right answer</h2>
  <ol>
    {steps.map((s) => (
      <li><strong>{s.name}.</strong> {s.text}</li>
    ))}
  </ol>
  <p class="sgfw-note">
    Building Department contact information is from the City of St. George's website, checked September 26, 2026: 61 S Main St,
    St. George, UT 84770; Monday to Friday, 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. Permits can be submitted online through the city's CityInspect portal.
  </p>

  <h2>Gate features to ask about</h2>
  <p>
    Whatever the requirement turns out to be, the gate is where pool fences succeed or fail in real life. A gate that
    someone props open is no barrier at all. When you talk to fence companies, ask them to describe in the quote:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Closing and latching.</strong> Pool gates are commonly expected to close and latch on their own. Ask how the hinges and latch do that, and how the design keeps working after years of sun and dust.</li>
    <li><strong>Latch placement.</strong> Where the latch sits, and whether small children can reach it.</li>
    <li><strong>Swing direction.</strong> Which way the gate opens relative to the pool.</li>
    <li><strong>Hardware and finish.</strong> Hot climates are hard on cheap latches and hinges. Ask about the hardware brand and coating.</li>
    <li><strong>Gaps.</strong> Spacing between pickets and between the bottom rail and the ground, and whether the design can be climbed.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Materials for a pool fence</h2>
  <p>
    Open-style ornamental iron or aluminum is a common pool-fence choice because it lets you watch the pool from the house and stands up to sun. Vinyl and block are also used. Each one is a
    different tradeoff between visibility, privacy, upkeep, and price. For cost planning, the dated per-foot ranges as of {COST_AS_OF} are
    {perFoot[0].range} for chain-link, {perFoot[1].range} for wood privacy, {perFoot[2].range} for vinyl privacy, and {perFoot[3].range} for ornamental iron. Those are general fence ranges. Pool-specific hardware
    and gates can move the price, so see the <a href="/fence-and-block-wall-cost-st-george-ut/">cost guide</a> for the method.
  </p>

  <h2>New pool or existing pool?</h2>
  <p>
    Timing matters. If you are building a new pool, plan the barrier alongside it and ask the city how it is reviewed. If you are adding or replacing a fence around an existing pool, tell the Building Department exactly that when you call, because requirements can differ.
    Either way, share the confirmed requirement with every fence company you get quotes from.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing a fence company for a pool project</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Ask whether they have installed pool enclosures before, and to explain how their design meets the requirement you confirmed.</li>
    <li>Check license status yourself with the <a href="https://commerce.utah.gov/dopl/" rel="noopener">Utah Division of Professional Licensing</a>, and confirm insurance directly.</li>
    <li>Get the gate and latch specification in writing.</li>
    <li>Ask who handles the permit, the HOA submittal, and any inspection.</li>
    <li>Call 811 (<a href="https://www.bluestakes.org/" rel="noopener">Blue Stakes of Utah</a>) before digging; the service asks for at least 3 business days of notice.</li>
  </ul>

  <p class="sgfw-note">
    This page is general guidance, not a code interpretation or safety certification. Requirements can change, so confirm them
    directly with the City of St. George before you build.
  </p>
